---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Privacy — Sealshot"
  description="What Sealshot and seal-shot.com do (and don't do) with your data."
>
  <section class="section wrap prose" aria-labelledby="privacy-title">
    <p class="kicker">Privacy policy</p>
    <h1 id="privacy-title">Privacy</h1>
    <!-- Bump this whenever the substance changes, not on wording tweaks. -->
    <p>Effective August 3, 2026.</p>

    <h2>The app</h2>
    <p>
      Sealshot processes everything on your Mac. Screenshots, OCR text,
      annotations, generated metadata, and search indexes never leave your
      device. The app requires no account and collects no telemetry or
      analytics — nothing about what you capture, or how you use Sealshot, is
      ever sent anywhere. The three downloads it does make are listed below.
    </p>
    <p>
      Sealshot makes exactly three network requests, and <strong>all three are
      downloads</strong>. None of them sends anything about you — no account, no
      identifier, no license key, no usage data, and never your content.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Update check.</strong> Once a day Sealshot reads a public version
        feed and downloads a new release if there is one. You can turn this off
        in <strong>Settings ▸ General</strong>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Revocation list.</strong> On launch, Sealshot downloads a small
        public file listing licenses that have been revoked — after a refund, for
        example. It is a plain download of the whole list, which Sealshot then
        checks <em>on your Mac</em>: it never asks a server about your license,
        and never sends your license ID. This follows the same setting as the
        update check, so turning that off turns this off too.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Enhanced redaction model.</strong> Only if you choose to enable
        it, and only once, with your explicit consent. The model then runs
        entirely on your Mac and you can remove it at any time.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Each of these is an ordinary file download, so the server sees what any
      download reveals — that some computer asked for a file — and nothing that
      identifies you or your copy of Sealshot. There is no request in which
      Sealshot reports anything back.
    </p>
    <p>
      One thing worth naming: when you click <strong>Donate…</strong> (or
      <strong>Buy</strong> in older versions), Sealshot opens this website in
      your browser. Some older versions also had a <strong>Renew</strong>
      button whose link carried your license ID and email address so a purchase
      could be matched to an existing license. Either way that is your browser,
      at your request — not Sealshot reporting in. Nothing is sent if you never
      click it, and the "I've donated" checkbox sends nothing at all: it is a
      setting on your Mac, and we never verify it.
    </p>

    <h2>This website</h2>
    <p>
      seal-shot.com is a static site and sets no cookies. It uses
      <a href="https://www.cloudflare.com/web-analytics/" rel="nofollow">Cloudflare
      Web Analytics</a>, which counts page views without cookies, without
      fingerprinting, and without building any profile of you. It records the
      page you visited, the referring site, and general details of your browser
      and country — never an identifier that follows you between visits or
      between sites. There is nothing here to opt out of, because nothing
      identifies you.
    </p>
    <p>
      The pages with a video on them — the home page and
      <a href="/download/">download</a> — show a still image and a play button,
      not a YouTube player. Nothing is requested from Google, and no cookie of
      theirs is set, unless you press play. Do press it and the player loads from
      <code>youtube-nocookie.com</code>, at which point YouTube can see that
      you are watching and may store data on your device under Google's own
      terms — which is why it waits for you to ask. The still is served from
      this site, so even the thumbnail costs you nothing.
    </p>

    <h2>Donating</h2>
    <p>
      Payments are handled by <a href="https://polar.sh" rel="nofollow">Polar</a>,
      our merchant of record. Your card details go to Polar and its payment
      processor and never reach us — we never see or store them. Polar holds
      your name, billing address, email address, and receipt history as the
      seller of record.
    </p>
    <p>
      For each donation we keep one record of our own — your name, email
      address, the date, and the order id — in Cloudflare's key-value storage,
      so support questions about a donation can be answered. We do not store
      your billing address, and nothing is emailed to you by us: Polar's
      receipt is the only mail. These records are never sold, shared, or used
      for marketing, and <a href="/support/">on request</a> we delete them.
    </p>
    <p>
      Licenses bought before Sealshot became donation-supported are still on
      file — name, billing address, email, license ID, and what was issued —
      because answering support questions about a past purchase requires them.
      The license file itself names its owner in signed text; that was the
      sharing deterrent of the old model, and it is why the file is worth
      treating like a receipt rather than attaching to a public bug report.
      These records are deletable on request too.
    </p>

    <h2>Feedback form</h2>
    <p>
      If you send feedback through the <a href="/support/">support form</a>, your
      message — and your name and email address, if you choose to include them —
      is emailed straight to our inbox by our own service, through
      <a href="https://resend.com" rel="nofollow">Resend</a>, the same provider
      that delivers licenses. Nothing is stored on the way: there is no database
      of submissions, so the message exists only as an email. It goes through no
      third-party form relay, and we use it only to respond to you and improve
      Sealshot. It is never sold or shared.
    </p>

    <h2>Newsletter</h2>
    <p>
      If you sign up for updates, your email address is stored with Kit, our
      email service, and used only to send Sealshot news, offers, and tips. Signup is
      double opt-in, every email includes an unsubscribe link, and your
      address is never sold or shared.
    </p>

    <h2>Questions</h2>
    <p>
      Anything unclear? <a href="/support/">Ask us</a> — privacy questions are
      always welcome.
    </p>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
